  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This recipe serves 4 as a side dish and takes about 35 minutes. It is a classic example of"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "nimono"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", Japanese simmered cooking, where the squash slowly absorbs the seasoned broth."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cut the"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "kabocha"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "into chunks with the skin still on. The skin holds the pieces together as they cook and adds colour to the finished dish. The flesh becomes very tender but the skin stays intact. Do not peel it."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "ingredientsList",
        "attrs": {
          "defaultServings": 4,
          "items": [
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "kabocha-squash",
              "amount": 600,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": "seeds removed, cut into 4cm chunks, skin on",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "dashi-stock",
              "amount": 400,
              "unit": "ml",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "soy-sauce-light",
              "amount": 3,
              "unit": "tbsp",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "mirin",
              "amount": 3,
              "unit": "tbsp",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "caster-sugar",
              "amount": 1,
              "unit": "tbsp",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Place the kabocha chunks skin-side down in a single layer in a large saucepan. They do not need to be in a perfectly tight layer."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "dashi"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "stock, soy sauce, mirin and sugar together in a jug. Pour over the squash."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Bring to a simmer over a medium heat. Place a cartouche (a circle of baking paper cut to fit inside the pan) directly on top of the squash. This keeps the liquid in contact with the squash and prevents it evaporating too fast."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Simmer on a low to medium heat for 20 to 25 minutes until the squash is tender when pierced with a skewer. The broth should reduce to a small amount of glossy sauce."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Leave to rest in the pan for 5 minutes before serving. The flavour improves as the squash cools slightly and absorbs more of the broth. Serve warm or at room temperature."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Squash is falling apart and turning to mush",
              "cause": "It cooked for too long or the heat was too high.",
              "fix": "Check from 18 minutes onwards. Kabocha cooks faster than butternut squash. Keep the simmer gentle."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Broth has evaporated before the squash is cooked",
              "cause": "The heat was too high or the cartouche was not used.",
              "fix": "Add 100ml of water or more dashi and reduce the heat. The cartouche helps keep moisture in."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Dish tastes too sweet",
              "cause": "The kabocha flesh itself is very sweet and the mirin adds more sweetness.",
              "fix": "Reduce the sugar to half a tablespoon next time. Add a little more soy sauce to balance."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Kabocha no nimono is a home cooking staple across Japan. It appears on the table as a side dish for weeknight dinners. It is also common in bento boxes and as part of the small dishes that make up a traditional Japanese meal. Kabocha squash has been grown in Japan since it was introduced from Cambodia via Portuguese traders in the sixteenth century. Its name in Japanese reflects that history."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}